<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seneca the Younger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 4 BCE – 65 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Lucius Annaeus Seneca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucius Annaeus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>, Seneca the Younger, was a Stoic philosopher, statesman and dramatist of Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, born at Corduba in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> and trained in rhetoric and philosophy. Exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> under the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> in 41 CE, he was recalled in 49 to tutor the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>, and after Nero became emperor in 54 he served as his adviser, with the praetorian prefect Burrus. His influence waned, and in 65 he was forced to take his own life for alleged complicity in the Pisonian conspiracy, of which he may have been innocent. His essays and 124 letters are among the most important sources for ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stoicism">Stoicism</a>, and his tragedies include Medea, Thyestes and Phaedra.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seneca-the-younger.jpg" alt="Seneca the Younger" width="330" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Calidius · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duble_herma_of_Socrates_and_Seneca_Antikensammlung_Berlin_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They appear in Greek and Latin sources in the first century CE, when Roman writers such as <b>Seneca</b>, Lucan and the Jewish historian Josephus begin to mention them as a new and strong power between the Don and the Caucasus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The writers of the following century, among them <b>Seneca</b>, Tacitus, Pliny the Elder and Juvenal, are often called the silver age.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The judge Lovato Lovati studied the metres of <b>Seneca</b>’s tragedies, and Albertino Mussato wrote a Latin tragedy in Seneca’s manner, the Ecerinis, on the tyranny of Ezzelino III da Romano, for which Padua crowned him with laurel in 1315.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Corduba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=40" aria-label="Corduba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4431 802 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Seneca the Younger"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4881 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4881 1072h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=40">What was here in 40 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Thus it is: we do not receive a short life, but we make it a short one, and we are not poor in days, but wasteful of them.&#8221;</p><footer>On the Shortness of Life, 1 &#183; tr. Aubrey Stewart, 1889 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/64576/pg64576-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Why do we complain of Nature? she has dealt kindly with us. Life is long enough, if you know how to use it.&#8221;</p><footer>On the Shortness of Life, 2 &#183; tr. Aubrey Stewart, 1889 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/64576/pg64576-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Unbroken prosperity cannot bear a single blow; but he who has waged an unceasing strife with his misfortunes has gained a thicker skin by his sufferings, yields to no disaster, and even though he fall yet fights on his knee.&#8221;</p><footer>On Providence, 2 &#183; tr. Aubrey Stewart, 1889 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/64576/pg64576-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The greatest remedy for anger is delay: beg anger to grant you this at the first, not in order that it may pardon the offence, but that it may form a right judgment about it: if it delays, it will come to an end.&#8221;</p><footer>On Anger, book 2, 29 &#183; tr. Aubrey Stewart, 1889 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/64576/pg64576-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A large part of mankind manufacture their own grievances either by entertaining unfounded suspicions or by exaggerating trifles. Anger often comes to us, but we often go to it.&#8221;</p><footer>On Anger, book 3, 12 &#183; tr. Aubrey Stewart, 1889 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/64576/pg64576-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We find many men ungrateful, yet we make more men so, because at one time we harshly and reproachfully demand some return for our bounty, at another we are fickle and regret what we have given…&#8221;</p><footer>On Benefits, book 1 &#183; tr. Aubrey Stewart, 1887 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3794/pg3794-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
